{#
   THE DELETE BUTTON — one destructive form for every admin list.

   Twenty-five tables each carried their own copy of this: same markup, same
   `btn-action btn-danger-admin`, same CSRF pattern, and the same
   `onsubmit="return confirm('…')"` inline handler with the sentence hardcoded
   in French inside an HTML attribute.

   Parameters:

     action   where to post (already through `path()`)
     token    the CSRF token name, e.g. `'delete_place_' ~ place.id`
     confirm  the question to ask, already translated
     label    the button's word, already translated

   ⚠️ The confirmation is `confirm_controller.js`, not `onsubmit`. Same
   behaviour, one definition, and the sentence is now a translated value instead
   of a string buried in an attribute where no catalogue could reach it.

   ⚠️ This is **not** the security control. The CSRF token below and the
   permission check on the route are. A confirmation guards against a slip of
   the mouse and nothing more — never let it be the only thing between a click
   and a deletion.

   🔴 **The including page MUST load Stimulus** — `{{ importmap('app') }}`, which
   `base.html.twig` and `base_public.html.twig` already emit. The `onsubmit`
   attribute this replaces needed no bundle at all, so dropping this partial onto
   one of the **54 templates that carry their own `<head>`** would silently
   delete without asking. Checked before converting: all four callers today
   extend `base.html.twig`. Check it again before adding a fifth.
#}
<form method="post" action="{{ action }}"
      data-controller="confirm"
      data-action="submit->confirm#ask"
      data-confirm-message-value="{{ confirm|default('')|e('html_attr') }}">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token(token) }}">
    {% if action_value|default('') %}<input type="hidden" name="action" value="{{ action_value }}">{% endif %}
    <button class="btn-action btn-danger-admin" type="submit">{{ label|default('common.delete'|trans) }}</button>
</form>
